<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waikato-Tainui</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Waikato-Tainui is a confederation of Māori iwi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s North Island, descended from those who arrived on the Tainui canoe. It led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maori-king-movement">Kīngitanga</a>, the Māori King Movement, from 1858, and lost much of its land in confiscations after the invasion of the Waikato in 1863. Its settlement with the Crown in 1995 included an apology and the return of land.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Waikato-Tainui belongs to the wider Tainui confederation of Polynesian settlers who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> on the Tainui waka, or migration canoe. The tribe takes its name from the Waikato River, which plays a large part in its history and culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a> is its historical centre and modern capital, while Hamilton City is now its largest population centre.</p><p>The first Māori king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potatau-te-wherowhero">Pōtatau Te Wherowhero</a>, was a member of the Ngāti Mahuta hapū of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a>, and his descendants have succeeded him. The King Movement is based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turangawaewae">Tūrangawaewae marae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a>. The Waikato iwi has long used the name Tainui for itself, notably through the Tainui Māori Trust Board, set up under the Waikato-Maniapoto Maori Claims Settlement Act 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Waikato-Tainui is made up of 33 hapū and 68 marae, with more than 80,000 tribal members affiliated to it; in the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> census 47,664 people gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> as an affiliation. Each tribal group has its marae, or meeting grounds, which usually include a wharenui, a meeting house. Among the groups are Ngāti Mahuta, associated with 20 marae, and Ngāti Te Wehi, Ngāti Hikairo, Ngāti Tāhinga, Ngāti Apakura and Ngāti Hauā.</p><p>The marae extend from Te Kūiti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in the south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> in the north. Many lie in and around Huntly and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a> and around the Kawhia, Aotea and Raglan harbours, and Te Puea Memorial Marae stands at Māngere Bridge.</p><p>The tribe's parliament is Te Kauhanganui, a body of 204 members, three from each of the 68 marae. Its executive board, Te Arataura, has ten representatives elected from Te Kauhanganui and an eleventh appointed by the Māori king. The tribal administration is the Waikato Raupatu Trustee Company Ltd at Hopuhopu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a>, which replaced the Tainui Māori Trust Board. The tribe has long had strong links with the University of Waikato, which holds documents and objects related to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Waikato Tainui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty settlement process is among the most extensive attempts by a settler society to address historical injustice: major settlements included the fisheries agreement of 1992, the <b>Waikato-Tainui</b> settlement of 1995 and the Ngāi Tahu settlement of 1998, each accompanied by a Crown apology or redress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
